    "finish": "stop",
    "reply": "The gene most directly associated with cystic fibrosis is CF transmembrane conductance regulator, identified as NCBIGene:1080 [1]. Two additional genes appear in the retrieved association set: Fc gamma receptor IIa (NCBIGene:2212) [3] and transforming growth factor beta 1 (NCBIGene:7040) [4]. The disease itself is recorded as Cystic fibrosis in the knowledge graph [2][6].\n\n## CFTR as the Primary Disease Gene\n\nCF transmembrane conductance regulator (NCBIGene:1080) is the core gene linked to Cystic fibrosis in the graph's gene-disease association records [1][2]. Mutations in this gene are the established molecular basis of the condition, and the disease concept is independently confirmed in both MedGen and the literature entity index under the name Cystic Fibrosis [6][7].\n\n## Modifier Genes in the Association Set\n\nBeyond CFTR, the graph returned two further gene nodes associated with the disease: Fc gamma receptor IIa (NCBIGene:2212) and transforming growth factor beta 1 (NCBIGene:7040) [3][4]. These genes are typically considered candidate modifiers of cystic fibrosis severity rather than causal loci, consistent with their known roles in immune regulation and tissue remodeling.\n\n## Clinical Context and Supporting Records\n\nThe MedGen record for Cystic fibrosis lists clinical features including asthma, bronchiectasis, clubbing of fingers, dehydration, diarrhea, hemoptysis, hepatomegaly, and hepatosplenomegaly [11][15][19][23][27][28][29][30]. Recent literature indexed under this disease covers topics such as nutrition, cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, azithromycin therapy, and paediatric care [10][14][18][22]. A case report describes hyperammonemic encephalopathy as a rare complication precipitated by corticosteroids in cystic fibrosis liver disease [5][26]. Clinical trials registered for cystic fibrosis include studies of quadriceps muscle electrostimulation and pregnancy outcomes [8][24].",
    "dur": 2.501
   },
   {
    "t": 8.28,
    "site": "write: synth",
    "role": "synth",
    "prompt_chars": 42978,
    "max_tokens": 4000,
    "ok": true,
    "prompt_tokens": 11622,
    "completion_tokens": 376,
    "cached_tokens": 10880,
    "finish": "stop",
    "reply": "Three genes are associated with cystic fibrosis: NCBIGene:1080, named CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]; NCBIGene:2212, named Fc gamma receptor IIa [3]; and NCBIGene:7040, named transforming growth factor beta 1 [4]. The disease itself is recorded as Cystic fibrosis [2].\n\n## Core Gene\n\nThe primary gene linked to cystic fibrosis is NCBIGene:1080, the CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]. Two additional genes, NCBIGene:2212 (Fc gamma receptor IIa) [3] and NCBIGene:7040 (transforming growth factor beta 1) [4], are also associated with the condition.\n\n## Disease Context\n\nCystic fibrosis is confirmed as a MedGen disease concept [6] and as a literature entity [7]. Clinical features documented for the disease include asthma [11], bronchiectasis [15], clubbing of fingers [19], dehydration [23], diarrhea [27], hemoptysis [28], hepatomegaly [29], and hepatosplenomegaly [30].\n\n## Literature and Trials\n\nRecent publications address encephalopathy in cystic fibrosis [5][26], nutrition [10], cystic fibrosis in children [14], azithromycin treatment [18], cystic fibrosis\u2013related diabetes [22], and a case of hyperammonemic encephalopathy precipitated by corticosteroids in cystic fibrosis liver disease [26]. Clinical trials include studies on quadriceps muscle electrostimulation [8] and pregnancies in cystic fibrosis patients [24]. Three trials addressing fibrocystic breast disease are also retrieved [12][16][20].",
    "dur": 1.951
   }
